#lang saffron
;; docs/reported/saffron-unannotated-param-container-cast-panics: passing a
;; container to an UNANNOTATED Saffron parameter.  The most ordinary thing a
;; program does, in the dialect whose whole point is that the parameter needs
;; no annotation -- and it panicked on the compiled path while the interpreter
;; was correct.
;;
;; `vec-get` is `[A] [v : (Vec A) i : int]`, so the D5 seam's target is
;; `(Vec A)` -- a TY_APP whose ARGUMENT is a tyvar.  The seam's exemption ("a
;; callee expecting a type variable has nothing to check against") tests the
;; KIND, so `(Vec A)` sailed past it and the seam checked the caller's `any`
;; against a `Vec` instantiation nothing had chosen:
;;
;;   panic: cast: any holds a different instantiation of Vec
;;
;; THIS FIXTURE ASSERTS VALUES, NOT THE ABSENCE OF A PANIC, and that is the
;; whole point.  The first attempt at this fix grounded the target to
;; `(Vec any)`, removed the panic, and printed the element's BOX ADDRESS
;; (94242104267456 for 7) -- a wrong answer, which is worse than the panic it
;; replaced.  It was reverted.  A fixture that only checked "does not crash"
;; would have passed against that revision.
;;
;; The missing half was that the call's tyvar bindings are collected from the
;; argument BEFORE the seam replaces it -- from `any`, which binds nothing --
;; so `A` stayed open, the result collapsed to the int64 carrier, and the
;; return position widened the box POINTER instead of reading through it.
;; Re-collecting after the substitution grounds `A := any` and the payload is
;; read.
;;
;; Floats carry a non-zero fractional part deliberately: an integer-valued
;; float cannot distinguish a payload from a truncation.

(defn pick  [v] (vec-get v 0))
(defn pick1 [v] (vec-get v 1))

(defn main []
  (println (cast (pick  [7 8]) int))
  (println (cast (pick1 [7 8]) int))
  (println (cast (pick  [7.1 8.25]) float))
  (println (cast (pick1 [7.1 8.25]) float))
  (println (cast (pick  ["ay" "bee"]) cstr))
  (println (cast (pick  [true false]) bool))
  ;; the tag and the payload must agree -- a box address would still tag `int`
  (println (type-of (pick [7.1 8.25])))
  (println (type-of (pick ["ay"])))
  0)
